---
// Two views of the eight index funds that reach SGX in October 2026.
//
//   What you own   — a look-through: the reader enters S$ amounts in any of the eight
//                    lines and sees how much of the whole sits in each company, counting
//                    only names in each fund's published top ten (DWS factsheets,
//                    30 September 2026), so the true figures can be slightly higher.
//   What it costs  — one exposure, several routes (the SGX line, the same fund overseas,
//                    a US-registered fund where one exists, a unit trust on a platform),
//                    bought once, held, sold once. Fund fee, dividend tax where it differs
//                    between routes, and the dealing costs the reader enters.
//
// Estimates under stated assumptions, not measured performance, and no verdict: rows stay
// in a fixed order and share one neutral colour. public/data/sgx-etf-menu.py reproduces the
// default outputs; if the arithmetic changes here, change it there.
import { Font } from 'astro:assets';
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';
import { getSgdPerUsd } from '@src/utils/fxRate';

const fx = await getSgdPerUsd();
---

<Font cssVariable="--font-mono" />

<div class="sm" data-card="tool">
  <div class="sm-head">
    <span class="sm-lbl">Eight new ETFs on SGX</span>
    <span class="sm-lbl accent card-live">Interactive</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sm-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Choose a view">
    <button type="button" class="sm-tab" id="sm-tab-own" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="sm-own">What you own</button>
    <button type="button" class="sm-tab" id="sm-tab-cost" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="sm-cost">What it costs</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ What you own ============ -->
  <section id="sm-own" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="sm-tab-own">
    <div class="sm-body">
      <p class="sm-hint">Enter what you hold or plan to hold in each fund, in Singapore dollars. Equal
      amounts are filled in as a hypothetical example, not a suggested portfolio. Leave a fund at 0 to exclude it. All four iFAST funds use published pre-launch TER figures, treated here as estimates. None is a guaranteed cap.</p>
      <div class="sm-tblwrap">
        <table class="sm-tbl" id="sm-own-tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Fund</th><th>Fee a year</th><th>Amount S$</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><!-- rows injected, fixed order --></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sm-bars-wrap">
      <div class="sm-bars-t" id="sm-own-t">&mdash;</div>
      <div class="sm-bars" id="sm-own-bars"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="sm-out"><div class="sm-out-s" id="sm-own-say">&mdash;</div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ What it costs ============ -->
  <section id="sm-cost" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="sm-tab-cost" hidden>
    <div class="sm-body">
      <p class="sm-hint">Japan: 0.43%; AI: 0.63% a year (FSM Global, data at 30 September 2026). Korea: 0.67%; Taiwan: 0.77% (same publisher, data at 29 September 2026). These pre-launch TER figures are estimates, not audited actual costs or guaranteed caps. Their breakdown is not supplied. Sources and assumptions are below.</p>
      <div class="sm-top">
        <label class="sm-f">
          <span>Market or theme</span>
          <select id="sm-exp" class="sm-sel" aria-label="Market or theme">
            <option value="XUS">S&amp;P 500</option>
            <option value="XWR">MSCI World</option>
            <option value="XND">Nasdaq-100</option>
            <option value="EUS">S&amp;P 500 Equal Weight</option>
            <option value="TWD">MSCI Taiwan</option>
            <option value="KOR">MSCI Korea</option>
            <option value="JPY">Nikkei 225</option>
            <option value="XBG">AI &amp; Big Data</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label class="sm-f">
          <span>Amount invested</span>
          <span class="sm-in"><i>S$</i><input type="text" id="sm-amt" value="10,000" inputmode="decimal" class="w-lg"></span>
        </label>
        <label class="sm-f">
          <span>Years invested</span>
          <span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-yrs" value="10" inputmode="decimal"><i>yrs</i></span>
        </label>
        <label class="sm-f" id="sm-dy-f">
          <span>Index dividends (%)</span>
          <span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-dy" value="1.04" inputmode="decimal"><i>%</i></span>
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="sm-acct" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Which money pays for it">
        <span class="sm-acct-l">Paid with</span>
        <button type="button" class="sm-pill" id="sm-cash" role="radio" aria-checked="true">Cash</button>
        <button type="button" class="sm-pill" id="sm-srs" role="radio" aria-checked="false">SRS money</button>
      </div>

      <details class="sm-more">
        <summary>Buying and selling costs: change these to your broker’s charges</summary>
        <p class="sm-hint">One purchase and one sale. The dividend input is annual index dividends as a percentage of its value. Trading fees apply to both exchanges; SGX trades also
        carry the exchange&rsquo;s clearing and access fees (0.0436% with GST), added automatically.
        Currency conversion is charged each way on any route not in Singapore dollars: about 0.5% at a
        bank-linked broker, 0.002% with a US$2 minimum at a specialist broker. The spread is the full gap
        between the buying and selling price; half of it is paid each way. This tool applies the conversion percentage only, without a minimum charge; the article table includes the specialist minimum.</p>
        <div class="sm-grid">
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Trading fee</span><span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-comm" value="0.08" inputmode="decimal"><i>%</i></span></label>
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Minimum trading fee</span><span class="sm-in"><i>S$</i><input type="text" id="sm-cmin" value="10" inputmode="decimal"></span></label>
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Currency conversion, each way</span><span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-fx" value="0.50" inputmode="decimal"><i>%</i></span></label>
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Yearly account charge overseas</span><span class="sm-in"><i>S$</i><input type="text" id="sm-cust" value="0" inputmode="decimal"><i>/yr</i></span></label>
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Unit trust fund fee</span><span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-utf" value="0.10" inputmode="decimal"><i>%</i></span></label>
          <label class="sm-f"><span>Unit trust platform fee</span><span class="sm-in"><input type="text" id="sm-utp" value="0.35" inputmode="decimal"><i>%</i></span></label>
        </div>
        <div class="sm-tblwrap">
          <table class="sm-tbl" id="sm-spread-tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Way to buy</th><th>Buy/sell gap %</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><!-- rows injected per exposure --></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="sm-bars-wrap">
      <div class="sm-bars-t" id="sm-cost-t">&mdash;</div>
      <div class="sm-bars" id="sm-cost-bars"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="sm-out"><div class="sm-out-s" id="sm-cost-say">&mdash;</div></div>
  </section>

  <div class="sm-notes">
    <details class="sm-more">
      <summary>How this is calculated</summary>
      <p><b>What you own.</b> Each company&rsquo;s share of your total = the sum over your lines of
      (amount in the line &times; the company&rsquo;s weight in that fund) &divide; your total. Weights are the
      funds&rsquo; published top-ten holdings at 30 September 2026 (DWS factsheets); a company outside a
      fund&rsquo;s top ten counts as zero there, so true figures can be slightly higher. The equal-weight
      fund holds about 500 names at roughly 0.2% each, taken as 0.2% for the US names shown. Samsung
      Electronics includes its preference shares. The combined fee weights each line&rsquo;s annual fund
      charge by amount. All four iFAST funds use FSM Global&rsquo;s published pre-launch TER figures: Japan/AI at 30 September 2026 and Korea/Taiwan at 29 September 2026. These are estimates, not caps.</p>
      <p><b>What it costs.</b> Running cost a year = fund fee + dividend tax where the routes differ
      (S&amp;P 500 only: 15% of the yield inside an Irish fund; 30% of the yield after fees on a US fund&rsquo;s
      distributions). For every other exposure the dividend tax is the same on each route shown and is left
      out. One-off = commission (or the minimum) + SGX fees on SGX routes + half the spread + currency
      conversion on non-S$ routes, paid to buy and again to sell. Custody is charged yearly on overseas
      holdings. The unit-trust row is fund fee + platform fee, with no dealing costs, and the same dividend
      treatment as an Irish fund. US estate tax on the US funds applies the published US schedule less the
      US$13,000 credit, for a holder who is neither a US citizen nor US-domiciled with no other US-situated
      assets, at S$1 = US${(1 / fx.sgdPerUsd).toFixed(4)} ({fx.source}, {fx.asOf}).</p>
    </details>
    <p><b>What this is not.</b> Advice or a ranking. Lines and routes stay in a fixed order; the numbers are
    facts about published holdings and fee schedules applied to your inputs.</p>
    <ToolBrand />
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
